{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "D-Mart Revenue Driver Tree and Cost Build Forecast",
  "scenario": "You are building a financial model for D-Mart (Avenue Supermarts) for FY26–FY30. The CFO has provided actuals through FY25 and wants a bottom-up revenue forecast driven by store rollforward and per-store productivity, then a cost build that reflects the company's operating leverage. Your task is to build the engine, compute implied metrics, and defend the forecast against sanity checks.",
  "instructions": "Build a store-driven revenue forecast using opening/new/closing store rolls and average store productivity, compute cost build lines anchored to historical percentages, calculate implied per-store revenue and market-share metrics, then identify forecast risks via sanity checks.",
  "learning_objective": "Construct a defendable revenue driver tree decomposing store growth and per-store productivity, apply multiplicative growth mechanics to validate the build, and recognise when a forecast implies unsustainable unit expansion or margin expansion.",
  "hints": [
    "Average stores = (Opening + Closing) / 2; it prevents over-crediting new openings. Closing FY25 (actual) becomes Opening FY26 (forecast).",
    "Revenue per store growth in estimates: Prior Year × (1 + productivity growth %) where productivity growth is a blue dial (say 5% annually). Multiply average stores × revenue per store to get revenue.",
    "Gross Profit = Revenue × Gross Margin%; COGS = Revenue − Gross Profit. EBITDA = Revenue − COGS − Employee costs − Other operating expenses. Use historical percentages as anchors (COGS ~65–68% of revenue for D-Mart, employees ~8–10%, other ops ~12–15%)."
  ],
